WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.940
The Batman versus Superman Easter Eggs That Flew Over Your Head

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:12.080
With a running time of two-and-a-half hours,
Batman versus Superman: Dawn of Justice has a lot

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.340
going on. We've gone over all 153 minutes
with a fine-toothed comb and picked out the

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.820
best and biggest Easter eggs to give you hints
of what to look for in the movie. Here's a

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.539
spoiler-filled guide to all the DC Comics
goodies peppered throughout the film. One

00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:28.490
more time, for anyone who's not paying attention…

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.039
Major spoilers ahead.

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:34.220
Still here? Great. Let's dive in.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.780
Darkseid is coming

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.530
Bruce Wayne's nightmare vision, which occurs
while he's asleep at his Bat-desk, hints at

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:44.379
something major looming on the DC movie horizon.
Fans of the comics will recognize the flying

00:00:44.379 --> 00:00:49.409
creatures in this scene as Parademons, the
nasty comics creatures who serve the intergalactic

00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:53.809
uber-villain known as Darkseid. You can also
see Darkseid's Omega symbol carved into the

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:57.690
ground at the beginning of Bruce's vision,
and Luthor seems to directly reference his

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.979
impending arrival when he threatens "he's
coming" at the end of the movie. But that's

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:06.250
not the only hints of Darkseid's presence
we get out of Batman v Superman...

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.520
Mother Box

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.460
When Wonder Woman takes a tour through Lex
Luthor's metahuman files, we get our best

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.460
looks yet at the future members of the Justice
League, including the Flash, Aquaman, and

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.250
Cyborg. In particular, Cyborg's origin, told
via lab footage that shows him being, uh,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.490
booted up for the first time, has even more
to offer fans than just the beginnings of

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:30.939
a major superhero. That's because we get a
look at what seems to be a Mother Box, a super-powerful

00:01:30.939 --> 00:01:36.069
supercomputer ripped right from the pages
of Jack Kirby's New Gods saga. Just like Batman's

00:01:36.069 --> 00:01:41.140
vision of the future, the Mother Box has major
ties with Darkseid and the aliens of New Genesis

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.509
and Apokalips. Since it looks like Cyborg's
creation has direct ties to New Gods technology—just

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:50.520
like the new version of his origin in the
comics from 2011— expect the hero to play

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.499
a pretty big role in the DC Extended Universe.

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:54.539
Riddle me this

00:01:54.539 --> 00:01:59.340
Batman's troubled past is felt more than seen
or discussed in Batman v Superman: Dawn of

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.729
Justice, but Snyder's fondness for referencing
comics lore can be seen in the building where

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:08.090
the showdown between the two heroes goes down.
If you look carefully, you can spot what looks

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:12.490
a lot like some graffiti left behind by the
Riddler, or perhaps some of his henchmen. Might

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:21.780
we see him in future DC movies? And will Jim
Carrey reprise the role? Eesh.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.640
KGBeast

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.540
Lex Luthor has a high-ranking henchman in
Batman v Superman named Anatoli Knyazev (played

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.660
by Callan Mulvey), and although he doesn't
look anything like he does in the comics,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.200
DC fans will recognize that as the real name
of the villain known as KGBeast. Could we

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.090
be seeing Knyazev in a different form later
on? Considering his fate in this movie—getting

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.970
exploded after Batman shoots at the flame-thrower
he's wearing on his back—it's a good bet

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.780
that he'll be more inclined to wear something
closer to his comic book costume in future

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:55.040
appearances. Assuming, of course, he hasn't
simply been incinerated, that is.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.340
Mercy Graves

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.700
That's no ordinary employee bringing Zod's
corpse to Lex Luthor. Fans of Superman: The

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.660
Animated Series will recognize the character
of Mercy Graves, played here by Tao Okamoto,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.760
Luthor's personal assistant and bodyguard.
She became a fan-favorite during the run of

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.330
the series, and it's exciting to see her brought
to the big screen in such a major movie. Of

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:18.480
course, unless we misunderstood the scene
where she's inside the Senate building that...explodes

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.910
about halfway through the movie, we might
not get much more Mercy in future DC Films

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:24.320
flicks. Ah, well.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:24.790
Metallo

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:30.500
Before the movie came out, it was rumored
that Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice would

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:35.020
feature an appearance by Metallo, the comics
villain who goes from life as a soldier to

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.700
a life of crime after having his body rebuilt
with Kryptonite-powered cyborg parts. That

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.720
didn't quite happen, probably because of how
many other details were crammed into its running

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.620
time. Yet if you watch the closing credits,
you'll see that one of the technicians demonstrating

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:53.220
the effects of Kryptonite in Luthor's lab
is named Emmet Vale…which just happens to

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.570
be the name of the scientist who built Metallo.
Watch for the metal man to make his debut

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:01.470
in a future DC Extended Universe film.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:02.540
Jimmy Olsen

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:06.970
As much fun as Zack Snyder clearly has calling
back to the comics, he isn't above changing

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.830
things up when it suits him. Case in point:
Jimmy Olsen's brief (and ultimately tragic)

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:16.609
cameo in Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice.
Early in the film, when Lois travels to the

00:04:16.609 --> 00:04:21.030
fictional country of "Nairomi, Africa" for
a story, she's accompanied by a photographer

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:25.650
who turns out to be a CIA spook and is quickly
put to death by the warlord she's interviewing.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.120
According to remarks Snyder made during his
press tour for the film, that's actually Jimmy

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.789
Olsen. Although Snyder didn't think there
was any room in the DC cinematic universe

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:37.439
for him, the director decided putting one
of Superman's best friends in the movie and

00:04:37.439 --> 00:04:42.280
killing him off would be a way to "have fun"
with the character.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:43.490
Zod of Kandor

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:48.379
We saw a return from Man of Steel's bad guy,
General Zod, albeit in the form of a corpse

00:04:48.379 --> 00:04:52.639
(and then a CGI monster). While the trailers
showed that to be the case months before the

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:56.659
movie dropped, the little robots inside the
Kryptonian ship did give us another cool little

00:04:56.659 --> 00:05:00.400
Easter egg. We find out that Zod comes from
the City of Kandor, which is better known

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.349
to comic fans as the Bottle City of Kandor.
The captial city of Krytpon, Kandor gets shrunken

00:05:05.349 --> 00:05:11.080
and stolen by the Superman villain Brainiac,
added to his intergalactic collection of artifacts.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.479
After Superman and Brainiac square off, Kandor
ends up under Superman's protection at the

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:19.529
Fortress of Solitude. Hopefully we'll have
a chance to see a movie version of the classic

00:05:19.529 --> 00:05:22.430
Superman villain with Kandor in tow.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:23.599
The Dark Knight Returns

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:28.599
If you've watched Watchmen, you know Zack
Snyder likes recreating comics panels in his

00:05:28.599 --> 00:05:33.520
film adaptations, and Batman v Superman: Dawn
of Justice is loaded with callbacks to one

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.310
of its biggest inspirations, the Frank Miller
graphic novel The Dark Knight Returns. It

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:42.210
starts early, with this movie's depiction
of Bruce Wayne's parents being killed (a shot

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.370
of Mrs. Wayne's pearls around the mugger's
gun is a direct lift from the book), and continues

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.860
throughout the movie, both in the dialogue
and in the way certain shots are lined up.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.650
More than anything, however, Batman's powered
armor, which he wears during the title fight

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.969
with Superman, is taken straight from Miller's
iconic series.

00:05:58.969 --> 00:05:59.729
Action Comics

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:05.719
Love him or hate him, Snyder definitely enjoys
paying homage to the comics. One brief example

00:06:05.719 --> 00:06:09.629
comes during an argument between Clark Kent
and Perry White at the office of the Daily

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:13.449
Planet, during which White tells Kent that
he can't report on the types of stories he

00:06:13.449 --> 00:06:18.749
wants to cover because "it's not 1938 anymore."
As any DC fan will tell you, that's the year

00:06:18.749 --> 00:06:22.159
Superman made his first appearance in Action
Comics #1.

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:23.800
Must There Be a Superman?

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.770
The movie's Action Comics reference is far
from the only nod to the Man of Steel's printed

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:33.469
adventures. During one scene, the senator
played by Holly Hunter asks, "Must there be

00:06:33.469 --> 00:06:37.960
a Superman?" It's an interesting question,
and one that directly points to a 1972 issue

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:42.689
of the Superman comic in which our hero is
forced to face a tribunal for his "crimes

00:06:42.689 --> 00:06:46.849
against humanity." Since the hero is instrumental
in the defeat of Doomsday and the foundation

00:06:46.849 --> 00:06:50.249
of the Justice League, this is a question
that gets answered with a resounding "yes"

00:06:50.249 --> 00:06:52.370
by the end of the movie.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:56.189
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:06:56.189 --> 00:06:59.819
just watched. And leave us a comment to let us
know which Easter egg was your favorite—and

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.860
any ones you think should've made the list!

